{
  "calculator": "budget",
  "slug": "snap-benefits-calculator",
  "title": "SNAP Benefits Calculator for a Monthly Food Budget",
  "metaDescription": "Estimate a full-month SNAP food benefit from household income and allowed deductions. See the federal math, current limits, and state-rule exceptions.",
  "targetKeyword": "snap benefits calculator",
  "h1": "Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) Benefits Calculator",
  "introText": "A Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits calculator estimates a full-month food benefit by subtracting 30% of your household's SNAP net income, rounded up, from the maximum allotment for your household size. At ModernWallet, we built this one on the federal fiscal year (FY) 2027 amounts, which apply from October 1, 2026, through September 30, 2027. The calculator opens on a hypothetical four-person household earning $2,000 a month with $1,200 in rent, and our arithmetic gives that household $766 a month if the state finds it eligible.",
  "howItWorks": "",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How SNAP Benefits Are Calculated",
      "body": "A SNAP benefit equals the maximum monthly allotment for your household size minus 30% of your household's net income, with that 30% rounded up to the next whole dollar. The [U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) Food and Nutrition Administration (FNA)](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/recipient/eligibility) bases the 30% share on the expectation that SNAP households spend about 30% of their own resources on food. For FY2027 in the 48 contiguous states and the District of Columbia, the maximum allotment runs from $306 for one person to $1,841 for eight.\n\nThe calculator above works through six steps in this order:\n\n1. Add earned income and unearned income, such as Social Security, to get gross monthly income.\n2. Subtract 20% of earned income.\n3. Subtract the standard deduction for your household size.\n4. Subtract dependent care, child support where your state allows it, and medical costs over $35 for an elderly or disabled member.\n5. Subtract the part of shelter costs above half of that adjusted income, up to $769 unless a member is elderly or disabled.\n6. Multiply net income by 0.3, round up, and subtract the result from the maximum allotment.\n\nThe calculator labels its result as conditional because the formula only pays out once a state agency finds the household eligible. When net income lands above the federal net income limit for your household size, the calculator holds back a benefit amount unless you confirm that a state agency has already found the household eligible.\n\nFor an eligible household of one or two people, FY2027 sets a $25 minimum benefit. The calculator shows that $25 floor on its own line, and it never turns a failed income screen into an award.\n\nThe calculator covers households of 1 to 8 people in the 48 contiguous states and the District of Columbia. Households in Alaska, Hawaii, Guam, or the U.S. Virgin Islands, and households of nine or more, get no estimate here and should use the [FY2027 allotment and deduction tables](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/allotment/cola) instead. You can copy or download the result with your inputs, and nothing you type is sent to a server."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which Income and Deductions to Enter",
      "body": "Enter your household's monthly gross income in two parts, earned and unearned, because the 20% deduction applies to earned income. Wages go in the earned field. Social Security goes in the unearned field.\n\nThen enter the deductions SNAP allows. FNA lists these for FY2027:\n\n- A standard deduction of $217 for 1 to 3 people, $229 for 4, $268 for 5, and $308 for 6 or more, which the calculator applies from your household size.\n- Dependent care costs when the care is needed for work, training, or education.\n- Legally owed child support payments, in states that allow this deduction.\n- Medical costs for an elderly or disabled member above $35 a month, when insurance or someone else does not pay them.\n- Excess shelter costs, explained below.\n\nShelter costs include rent or mortgage payments, mortgage interest, and taxes on the home, so put property taxes in the housing field if you pay them. Allowable utility costs are heating and cooking fuel, electricity, water, and the basic fee for one telephone.\n\nSome states allow a set amount for utilities in place of actual bills. FNA does not publish one national figure for that set amount, so the utility field starts at $0. Enter either your actual monthly bills or the amount your [state SNAP agency](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/state-directory) approves.\n\nHouseholds experiencing homelessness can get a standard $205.66 homeless shelter deduction. The calculator has no field for it, so ask your agency through the [SNAP state directory](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/state-directory) how it applies to your case."
    },
    {
      "heading": "SNAP Income Limits Are a Federal Reference",
      "body": "SNAP income limits for FY2027 set gross monthly income at 130% of the federal poverty level and net monthly income at 100%, and most households must stay under both. FNA publishes these limits for the 48 contiguous states, the District of Columbia, Guam, and the U.S. Virgin Islands. Alaska and Hawaii have higher limits.\n\n| Household size | Gross monthly limit (130%) | Net monthly limit (100%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | $1,729 | $1,330 |\n| 2 | $2,345 | $1,804 |\n| 3 | $2,960 | $2,277 |\n| 4 | $3,575 | $2,750 |\n| 5 | $4,191 | $3,224 |\n| 6 | $4,806 | $3,697 |\n| 7 | $5,421 | $4,170 |\n| 8 | $6,037 | $4,644 |\n| Each additional member | +$616 | +$474 |\n\nGross income is the household's total non-excluded income before any deductions. Net income is gross income minus the allowed deductions from the six calculator steps.\n\nThe table works as a reference line because state rules can change the result. A household where every member receives Temporary Assistance for Needy Families (TANF), Supplemental Security Income (SSI), or in some places general assistance may be categorically eligible. Most states also use broad-based categorical eligibility (BBCE), which lets a state align SNAP income and resource limits with its TANF-funded programs.\n\nUnder BBCE, a household still needs income low enough to produce a SNAP benefit and must still meet the non-financial rules. That is why the calculator lets you confirm a state eligibility determination when your net income sits above the federal figure."
    },
    {
      "heading": "SNAP Benefits for Older or Disabled Members",
      "body": "SNAP changes the benefit math in three ways when a household includes a member who is elderly or disabled:\n\n- The household only has to meet the net income limit, so the gross income test does not apply.\n- Medical costs above $35 a month that insurance or someone else does not pay count as a deduction.\n- The excess shelter deduction has no $769 cap, so all shelter costs above half of adjusted income come off.\n\nTurn on the elderly or disabled setting in the calculator to apply the medical deduction and the uncapped shelter deduction. Our arithmetic shows the shelter difference for the four-person household from the worked example with shelter costs raised to $1,600. Without an elderly or disabled member, the shelter deduction stops at $769 and the benefit is $842.\n\nWith an elderly or disabled member, the full $914.50 of excess shelter comes off. Net income drops to $456.50, 30% rounds up to $137, and the benefit becomes $886.\n\nHouseholds with a member age 60 or older or disabled can also hold up to $4,750 in countable resources, compared with $3,000 for other households. A person 60 or older who cannot buy and prepare meals separately because of a permanent disability may form a separate SNAP household with a spouse. That applies only when the others they live with have income no higher than 165% of the poverty level, which is $4,538 a month for a household of four in FY2027."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A Full-Month SNAP Worked Example",
      "body": "Our worked example gives a four-person household with $2,000 in monthly earnings and $1,200 in allowed shelter costs a full-month SNAP benefit of $766. The household has no unearned income, dependent care, child support, medical costs, or elderly or disabled member. The second column raises shelter costs to $1,400, and both columns are our own arithmetic on the FY2027 figures.\n\n| Step | $1,200 shelter | $1,400 shelter |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gross earned income | $2,000.00 | $2,000.00 |\n| 20% earned income deduction | $400.00 | $400.00 |\n| Standard deduction (4 people) | $229.00 | $229.00 |\n| Adjusted income | $1,371.00 | $1,371.00 |\n| Half of adjusted income | $685.50 | $685.50 |\n| Excess shelter deduction | $514.50 | $714.50 |\n| Net income | $856.50 | $656.50 |\n| 30% of net income, rounded up | $257 | $197 |\n| Maximum allotment (4 people) | $1,023 | $1,023 |\n| Full-month benefit | $766 | $826 |\n\nRaising allowed shelter costs by $200 raised the benefit by $60. Each added dollar of excess shelter lowers net income by a dollar and the household's 30% share by about 30 cents. The shelter deduction stops at $769 once shelter costs pass $1,454.50 ($685.50 plus $769), and from that point this household's benefit stays at $842 however high rent goes.\n\nThe household also clears both federal income references. Its $2,000 gross is under the $3,575 limit for four people, and its $856.50 net is under $2,750.\n\nWe did not copy the example on FNA's eligibility page. That example subtracts a $217 standard deduction for four people while the FY2027 deduction table lists $229, and its adjusted income changes from $1,171 to $1,165 between steps.\n\nThese are full-month amounts. If a state finds the household eligible, benefits count from the date the application was submitted, so the first month's payment can differ from the full-month figure."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What the Estimate Cannot Decide",
      "body": "The calculator runs the federal benefit formula and cannot decide whether your household is eligible for SNAP. Only the state agency where you live makes that decision, after an interview and proof of the information you give. The formula does not check these rules:\n\n- Countable resources, capped at $3,000, or $4,750 with a member age 60 or older or disabled. A home and lot, most retirement and pension plans, and resources of people receiving SSI or TANF do not count. Vehicles count, and each state decides how.\n- College students ages 18 through 49 enrolled at least half time, who are generally not eligible unless they meet specific exemptions.\n- Work requirements and non-citizen eligibility, which the Working Families Tax Cuts Act changed. The [FNA eligibility page](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/recipient/eligibility) says it is being updated with those changes.\n- Household membership. People who live together and buy and prepare meals together form one household, and spouses and most children under 22 count in the same household even if they shop and cook separately.\n\nYour estimate changes if your state uses a set utility amount, allows the child support deduction, or applies BBCE limits. It also changes on October 1, 2027, when FNA adjusts maximum allotments, deductions, and income standards for the next fiscal year.\n\nIf the state denies your application or sets a benefit you disagree with, you can request a fair hearing within 90 days of the decision. You can make that request by phone, in writing, or in person at the local SNAP office."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Your Next Step",
      "body": "Save the calculator's download with your inputs, then apply through the SNAP agency in the state where you live. Each state has its own application form, and FNA does not process applications or see case information, so use the [SNAP state directory](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/state-directory) to find your state's office, website, or hotline. In most cases, the state agency sends an eligibility notice within 30 days, and a household with less than $100 in liquid resources and $150 in monthly gross income may get benefits within 7 days.\n\nApproved benefits load each month onto an Electronic Benefit Transfer (EBT) card that works like a debit card. SNAP [can buy food for the household](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/eligible-food-items), including produce, meat, dairy, bread, snack foods, and seeds that grow food. It cannot buy alcohol, tobacco, vitamins, supplements, hot foods, pet food, cleaning supplies, paper products, or hygiene items.\n\nTreat the SNAP amount as a grocery fund and keep it out of your cash take-home pay when you [plan the rest of your household budget](/budget/household-budget-calculator/). If you have never tracked spending, [build a budget from your statements](/guides/how-to-make-a-budget/) first so the grocery line reflects what you buy. Then run your own numbers in the SNAP benefits calculator above and take the downloaded result to your state application."
    }
  ],
