  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do they calculate how much food stamps I get?",
      "answer": "Your state SNAP agency subtracts allowed deductions from your gross income to get net income, multiplies net income by 0.3, rounds up, and subtracts that amount from the maximum allotment for your household size. For a four-person household with $856.50 in net income, that is $1,023 minus $257, or $766 for a full month."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the minimum SNAP benefit?",
      "answer": "The FY2027 minimum SNAP benefit is $25 a month for eligible one- and two-person households in the 48 contiguous states and the District of Columbia. It is $31 to $49 in Alaska depending on area, $40 in Hawaii, $36 in Guam, and $32 in the U.S. Virgin Islands. The minimum applies only after a household is found eligible."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much can you get in SNAP benefits?",
      "answer": "For FY2027 in the 48 contiguous states and the District of Columbia, the most a household can get is $306 for one person, $562 for two, $808 for three, $1,023 for four, $1,217 for five, $1,463 for six, $1,616 for seven, and $1,841 for eight. Households of 9 to 17 add $225 per extra person. Your amount is that maximum minus 30% of your net income, rounded up, so a household with $0 net income gets the full maximum."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are SNAP benefits income limits?",
      "answer": "For FY2027, most households in the 48 contiguous states and the District of Columbia need gross monthly income under 130% of the poverty level and net income under 100%. For four people, that is $3,575 gross and $2,750 net. Households with an elderly or disabled member only need to meet the net limit, and states using broad-based categorical eligibility can align limits with their TANF-funded programs."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can seniors get food stamps?",
      "answer": "Yes, people age 60 or older can get SNAP when their household meets the program rules, and special rules apply to them. A household with a member who is 60 or older or disabled only has to meet the net income limit, can deduct medical costs over $35 a month, and has no cap on the excess shelter deduction. It can also hold $4,750 in countable resources, compared with $3,000 for other households."
    }
